    {
      "id": "997",
      "type": "writeup",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 997,
        "body_ja": "",
        "body": "<p>ぞ is a sentence-ending particle that is used for emphasis in a similar way to よ. However, it is quite a bit stronger and primarily only used by men. While よ may simply state a point or a fact, ぞ is usually reserved for more forcefully highlighting something. In many cases, it will just be translated as an exclamation point.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>ぞ may be used at the end of any sentence, but can only follow conjugatable words such as verbs, adjectives, and auxiliary verbs.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n <li data-study-question='155281'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155282'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155283'></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>The speaker frequently uses ぞ simply to encourage or convince themselves, rather than specifically directing the statement at another.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='155284'></ul>\r\n\r\n<section class='caution'><h4>Caution</h4>\r\n<p>Although uncommon, ぞ may also be used as an adverbial particle in the same way as は. In sentences like this, it is more emphatic and draws extra attention to the topic itself.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184628'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184629'></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Additionally, this use of ぞ as an adverbial particle may also follow question words such as どこ ‘where’, 誰（だれ） ‘who’, なに ‘what’, etc.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184630'>\r\n<li data-study-question='184631'></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>This use is also emphatic, but not quite to the same extent as with standard nouns.</p></section>"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1331",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1331,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "よ is a sentence-ending particle used to add emphasis and indicate the speaker is sharing something new (e.g., 面白いですよ = 'It's interesting, I'm telling you!'). ぞ is a rougher, more masculine sentence-ending particle that adds strong emphasis. It has a blunt tone and should be avoided in polite conversation or with strangers, as it lacks the softness or friendliness of よ.",
